{
  "id": "grammar.a1.138.b",
  "title": "When does it leave? When does it arrive?",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Indul and érkezik",
      "content": "To ask about a bus or a train, use *indul* (leaves) and *érkezik* (arrives). The bus is \"it\", so both are in the *ő* form with no extra ending. *Érkezik* is an *-ik* verb like *dolgozik*, so for \"I\" it is *érkezem*, not *érkezek*. *Mikor?* asks when, and *hamarosan* answers: soon."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "title": "Leaves, arrives",
      "rows": [
        [
          "indul",
          "leaves"
        ],
        [
          "érkezik",
          "arrives"
        ],
        [
          "Mikor indul a busz?",
          "When does the bus leave?"
        ],
        [
          "Mikor érkezik a vonat?",
          "When does the train arrive?"
        ],
        [
          "Hamarosan.",
          "Soon."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "title": "Examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "spanish": "Mikor indul a busz?",
          "english": "When does the bus leave?"
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Hamarosan indul.",
          "english": "It leaves soon."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Mikor érkezik a vonat?",
          "english": "When does the train arrive?"
        },
        {
          "spanish": "A vonat hamarosan érkezik.",
          "english": "The train arrives soon."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Mi hamarosan érkezünk.",
          "english": "We arrive soon."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "*Hamarosan* is \"soon\", not \"now\". For \"now\" you already know *most*. And remember the *-ik* verb: the bus *érkezik*, but I say *érkezem*."
    }
  ]
}
